      ":question/problem": "Find the **positive** $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3 x^{3} - 42 x - 4$ whose tangent has a slope of $-6.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=3 x^{3} - 42 x - 4.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f^{\\prime}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime}(x) &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{3} - 42 x - 4\\right) \\\\\n&= (3)\\cdot 3\\cdot x^{2} + (-42)\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 9 x^{2} - 42\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f^{\\prime}(x)=-6$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 x^{2} - 42 &= -6 \\\\\n9 x^{2} - 36 &= 0 \\\\\n9 \\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(x + 2\\right) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2,\\,2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$, the required coordinate is $x=2.$",
