    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6aa3f0e0-f21b-5655-ae61-ac9ad00f5b6c",
      ":question/problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3 x^{3} + 9 x^{2} - 4$ whose tangent has a slope of $-9.$\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=3 x^{3} + 9 x^{2} - 4.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f^{\\prime}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{\\prime}(x) &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 x^{3} + 9 x^{2} - 4\\right) \\\\\n&= (3)\\cdot 3\\cdot x^{2} + (9)\\cdot 2\\cdot x \\\\\n&= 9 x^{2} + 18 x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f^{\\prime}(x)=-9$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9 x^{2} + 18 x &= -9 \\\\\n9 x^{2} + 18 x + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n9 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-9$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
